WEBVTT

o0.1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:07.083
This video shows how yield in doeff, a library for algebraic effects in Python, differs from async and await.

o0.2
00:00:07.083 --> 00:00:11.917
We follow one small function that reads a name and returns a greeting: greet.

o0.3
00:00:11.917 --> 00:00:14.300
We write it two ways and compare them.

o1.1
00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:18.817
Python already has functions that pause: async def and await.

o1.2
00:00:18.817 --> 00:00:23.500
doeff pauses with yield instead. Both print the same line. So why yield?

o2.1
00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:27.750
At an await, the call names the code that answers.

o2.2
00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:31.900
That code runs right there, as part of the same chain of calls.

o3.1
00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:38.450
At a yield, the call names an operation. ReadName is a value, not code.

o3.2
00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:42.700
greet pauses, and the nearest handler around it answers.

o4.1
00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:48.317
In asyncio, every request goes to one place: the event loop at the top.

o4.2
00:00:48.317 --> 00:00:51.400
The coroutines in between only pass it through.

o5.1
00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:57.500
In doeff, each caller installs handlers around any part of a program.

o5.2
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:03.500
Add titled in between, and the first greeting says Dr. Ada. greet does not change.

o6.1
00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:07.933
A handler also holds the rest of the function, k.

o6.2
00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:12.350
It can resume it, keep it to run tasks, or end the run without it.

o7.1
00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:17.483
Async libraries still work. Await hands one coroutine to a handler,

o7.2
00:01:17.483 --> 00:01:21.750
and the event loop moves from above the program to behind that handler.

o8.1
00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:27.850
async and await are an effect system with one handler, fixed at the top.

o8.2
00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:30.650
doeff lets every caller install their own.
